<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Amiens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1802">1802</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Amiens · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>, signed in March 1802 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a>, briefly ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>. Britain returned most of its conquests but kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, taken from Spain, and the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> possessions on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>. The peace collapsed in May 1803, when Britain declared war again, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens.jpg" alt="Treaty of Amiens" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jules-Claude Ziegler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Paix_d%27Amiens_de_Jules-Claude_Ziegler_(1853).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-second-coalition">War of the Second Coalition</a> began well for the coalition, but after French victories at Marengo and Hohenlinden Austria eventually signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> in 1801, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the only major power still at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Horatio Nelson's victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> on 2 April 1801 halted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-armed-neutrality">League of Armed Neutrality</a>. Napoleon Bonaparte had made truce proposals as early as 1799, but the foreign secretary Lord Grenville and Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger">William Pitt the Younger</a> rejected them. When Pitt resigned in February 1801 over domestic issues, the more accommodating Henry Addington replaced him.</p><p>Addington's foreign secretary, Lord Hawkesbury, opened talks with the French commissary Louis Guillaume Otto, and preliminaries were signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 1 October 1801. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-cornwallis-1st-marquess-cornwallis">Lord Cornwallis</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in November to negotiate the final text against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a>, who kept shifting their positions. Pressure on Cornwallis was great, since the British public expected peace and Parliament was discussing the budget. The chief difficulty was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty called for the restoration of prisoners and hostages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was to return the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> and most of Dutch Guiana to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> and withdraw its forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, while the Batavian Republic ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>. Spain accepted British rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a> went back to Spain. France was to withdraw from the Papal States and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and the Septinsular Republic was recognised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gozo">Gozo</a> and Comino were to be restored to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> and declared neutral, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a> was to be compensated for its losses in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>News of the peace was greeted with celebration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and upper-class British visitors flocked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in the second half of 1802. Among them were Charles James Fox, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-m-w-turner">J. M. W. Turner</a> and William Hazlitt, who spent much of three months copying Italian masters in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. Napoleon used the interlude for reforms, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a> and the Concordat with the Vatican, and for a constitution that gave him lifetime control.</p><p>The peace did not last. The British objected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s exclusion of their trade from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and its client states, and to his reordering of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, while Napoleon objected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s refusal to evacuate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>. Addington kept a large peacetime army of 180,000 and used the interlude to rebuild British strength. Britain declared war on France on 18 May 1803, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> began.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Amiens” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Peace of Amiens</b> returned the Cape to the Batavian Republic in 1803, but a British force under General David Baird landed again and won the Battle of Blaauwberg on 8 January 1806, and the Netherlands formally ceded the colony in the Anglo-Dutch convention of 1814.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In February 1797 a British force under Sir Ralph Abercromby captured the island almost without resistance, and the <b>Treaty of Amiens</b> in 1802 confirmed the cession.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-trinidad">Colony of Trinidad</a></li><li><blockquote>They were formally ceded by the <b>Treaty of Amiens</b> in 1802 and became British Ceylon.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-ceylon">Dutch Ceylon</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain ceded Trinidad at the <b>Treaty of Amiens</b> in 1802, and it became the Colony of Trinidad.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-trinidad">Spanish Trinidad</a></li></ul></section>
